  "question": {
    "text": "Neopentyl bromide, undergoes dehydrohalogenation to give alkenes even though it has no <math> <mrow> <mtext>β</mtext><mo>−</mo><mtext>H</mtext></mrow> </math>. This is due to"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math> <mrow>  <msub>   <mtext>E</mtext>   <mtext>2</mtext>  </msub>  </mrow></math> mechanism",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Rearrangement of carbocations by <math> <mrow>  <msub>   <mtext>E</mtext>   <mtext>1</mtext>  </msub>  </mrow></math> mechanism",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math> <mrow>  <msub>   <mtext>E</mtext>   <mtext>1</mtext>  </msub>  <mtext>cB</mtext></mrow></math> mechanism",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math> <mrow>  <msub>   <mtext>E</mtext>   <mtext>4</mtext>  </msub>  </mrow></math> mechanism",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\st0006_a4bb47b06989.png\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 168px;\" /><br />Neopentyl bromide give a carbocation as intermediate which undergo for rearrangement and show <math> <mrow> <msub> <mtext>E</mtext> <mtext>1</mtext> </msub> </mrow> </math> mechanism (even though it has no <math> <mrow> <mtext>β</mtext><mo>−</mo><mtext>H</mtext></mrow> </math> atom).<br /><br />Thus Rearrangement of carbocations by <math> <mrow>  <msub>   <mtext>E</mtext>   <mtext>1</mtext>  </msub>  </mrow></math> mechanism is the correct option."
  }
